module gunbc.scm.proposal

import std.types { List }
import v2.std.node { Node, Symbol }
import v2.std.dependency { DependencyKind }

// THIS IS THE PROPOSAL AUTHORITY, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT NAMED AFTER AN OPERATION.
//
// These nouns previously lived in `gunbc.scm.merge`, which made an OPERATION module the home of the
// vocabulary its callers needed. That was measurable rather than a matter of taste: of the eight
// modules importing that module, SIX consumed only these nouns and never the operation, and three of
// those six were production modules -- gunbc.scm.status, gunbc.scm.authoring and
// gunbc.scm.read_command. A vocabulary with three times the consumers of the operation it was filed
// under is not a detail of that operation.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE MAY NOT BECOME. It is THE proposal authority, not one of two. A richer native
// proposal -- one carrying authored parent, target, occurrence, frame, authority, effects and
// evidence -- must WIDEN OR REPLACE these carriers in place. It must not arrive beside them as a
// second `NativeProposal` while this `Proposal` stays independently authoritative for the same
// transaction, because that is the section 3 fork this cut exists to remove, re-created one layer
// down.
//
// WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DEPEND ON: `ObjectStore` and `ObjectId`. A requirement is authored
// syntax; storage identity is not part of authoring it. Keeping that dependency out is what lets
// status, authoring and read-command consume this vocabulary without acquiring a storage fact none
// of them needs, and it fixes the direction of the graph -- proposal nouns, then integration, then
// the object store, never back the other way.

// A REQUIREMENT IS AUTHORED, AND ABSENCE IS ONE OF THE THINGS THAT CAN BE AUTHORED. A
// dependency-derived frontier can answer "is removing f compatible with everything that depends on
// f", but it cannot express "the author requested that f be absent" -- that is a stated requirement,
// not a consequence. Modelling delete as the absence of a positive requirement would make it
// indistinguishable from silence, and silence means PRESERVE THE TARGET, so a delete expressed by
// omission would be read as "leave f alone" -- the exact inversion of what was asked.
type Requirement
  = RequireBinding { role: Symbol, value: Node }
  | RequireBindingAbsent { role: Symbol }

fn requirement_role(r: Requirement) -> Symbol {
  match r {
    RequireBinding { role: role, value: _ } => role
    RequireBindingAbsent { role: role } => role
  }
}

type Proposal {
  requirements: List<Requirement>
  dependencies: List<RoleDependency>
}

// DEPENDENCIES ARE ROLE-GRAIN, NOT NODE-GRAIN, and that is a correctness choice rather than a
// convenience. `DependencyView` endpoints are Nodes, and a replacement mints a NEW immutable node --
// so a target-read edge `F1 -> K` says nothing about how `F2` relates to `K` once the binding is
// replaced. The binding ROLE is the bridge that survives the replacement, so the relation is carried
// at role grain and the question "does F2 still satisfy what K needed" stays answerable without
// giving nodes a temporal identity they must not have.
//
// AND IT IS NAMED FOR THE RELATION, NOT FOR EITHER OF ITS POPULATIONS. It was `MergeDependency`,
// which tied a neutral carrier to one operation. `ProposalDependency` was considered and REFUSED:
// the integration consumes this same type in TWO populations -- the target's own dependencies and
// the ones a proposal declares -- and combines them, so a proposal-flavoured name would make every
// target-side use a lie. What is genuinely operation-specific is which KINDS an integration
// supports, which kinds propagate a removal, and how an unsupported kind becomes a refusal. Those
// are classifier policy and they stay with the operation; none of them is a property of this row.
type RoleDependency {
  dependent: Symbol
  required: Symbol
  kind: DependencyKind
  usage_site: Symbol
}
